A reverse proxy sits in front of backend servers, forwarding client requests and providing SSL termination, load balancing, and caching.

**Basic reverse proxy** (forward all traffic to a backend app):
```nginx
# /etc/nginx/sites-available/app.conf
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.company.com;
    return 301 https://$server_name$request_uri;  # Redirect HTTP to HTTPS
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name app.company.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/app.company.com.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/app.company.com.key;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
    ssl_ciphers HIGH:!aNULL:!MD5;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;  # Backend app
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;

        # WebSocket support
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
    }
}
```

**Multiple backends** (route by path):
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name company.com;

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080/;  # API server
    }

    location /app/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000/;  # Web app
    }

    location / {
        root /var/www/html;  # Static files
    }
}
```

**Setup steps**:
```bash
# Install Nginx
sudo apt install nginx

# Create site config
sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/app.conf

# Enable the site
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/app.conf /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/

# Test configuration
sudo nginx -t

# Reload
sudo systemctl reload nginx
```

**Benefits of reverse proxying**:
- **SSL termination**: handle HTTPS at Nginx, backend uses HTTP.
- **Security**: hide backend server details, add rate limiting, block malicious requests.
- **Performance**: enable gzip compression, static file caching, connection pooling.
- **Flexibility**: change backend servers without changing the public URL.

**Rate limiting** (prevent abuse):
```nginx
http {
    limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=api:10m rate=10r/s;
}
server {
    location /api/ {
        limit_req zone=api burst=20 nodelay;
        proxy_pass http://backend;
    }
}
```

PowerShell has robust error handling with try/catch/finally.

```powershell
try {
    # Set errors to terminating so catch works
    $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'

    # Connect to a remote machine
    $session = New-PSSession -ComputerName 'Server01'
    Invoke-Command -Session $session -ScriptBlock {
        Get-Service -Name 'Spooler' | Restart-Service
    }
    Write-Host 'Service restarted successfully' -ForegroundColor Green
}
catch [System.Management.Automation.Remoting.PSRemotingTransportException] {
    Write-Error "Cannot connect to remote machine: $_"
}
catch {
    Write-Error "Unexpected error: $($_.Exception.Message)"
    # Log to file
    $_ | Out-File -Append 'C:\Logs\errors.log'
}
finally {
    # Always runs — cleanup
    if ($session) { Remove-PSSession $session }
}
```

Key concepts:
• `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'` makes non-terminating errors catchable.
• `$_` in catch is the error object.
• `$_.Exception.Message` gets the error message.
• Use `-ErrorAction Stop` on individual cmdlets instead of global preference.

A reverse proxy sits in front of web servers and forwards client requests to them:

**What it does**:
- **Load balancing**: Distributes traffic across multiple backend servers.
- **SSL termination**: Handles HTTPS encryption, so backend servers deal with plain HTTP.
- **Caching**: Stores frequently requested content to reduce backend load.
- **Security**: Hides backend server details, provides WAF capabilities.
- **Compression**: Compresses responses before sending to clients.

**Popular reverse proxies**:
- **Nginx**: Most popular. Lightweight, fast, great for static content.
- **HAProxy**: Best for pure load balancing. Very reliable.
- **Caddy**: Automatic HTTPS with Let's Encrypt. Simple config.
- **Traefik**: Designed for containers/microservices. Auto-discovery.
- **Apache**: mod_proxy. Often already installed.

**Nginx example**:
```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.example.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
```

**With SSL (Nginx)**:
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name app.example.com;
    ssl_certificate /etc/ssl/certs/app.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/ssl/private/app.key;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:3000;
    }
}
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.example.com;
    return 301 https://$server_name$request_uri;
}
```

**Caddy** (automatic HTTPS):
```
app.example.com {
    reverse_proxy localhost:3000
}
```
That's it — Caddy automatically obtains and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate.

Azure Blob Storage stores unstructured data — files, images, backups, logs.

**Create via CLI:**
```bash
# Create storage account
az storage account create --name mystorageacct --resource-group myRG --location eastus --sku Standard_LRS

# Create a container
az storage container create --name uploads --account-name mystorageacct

# Upload a file
az storage blob upload --container-name uploads --file ./report.pdf --name reports/2024/report.pdf --account-name mystorageacct

# List blobs
az storage blob list --container-name uploads --account-name mystorageacct --output table

# Download
az storage blob download --container-name uploads --name reports/2024/report.pdf --file ./downloaded.pdf --account-name mystorageacct
```

**Access tiers:**
• **Hot**: frequently accessed data. Highest storage cost, lowest access cost.
• **Cool**: infrequently accessed (30+ days). Lower storage, higher access cost.
• **Archive**: rarely accessed (180+ days). Cheapest storage, hours to rehydrate.

**Lifecycle management** (auto-tier):
```json
{
  "rules": [{
    "name": "archive-old-data",
    "type": "Lifecycle",
    "definition": {
      "actions": {
        "baseBlob": {
          "tierToCool": {"daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 30},
          "tierToArchive": {"daysAfterModificationGreaterThan": 180}
        }
      }
    }
  }]
}
```

Secure with: SAS tokens (temporary access), private endpoints, encryption at rest (default), and RBAC.

Here's a simple and practical script:

```powershell
# Check and restart a Windows service
# Change 'Spooler' to whatever service you want to monitor

$serviceName = 'Spooler'
$service = Get-Service -Name $serviceName -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

if ($null -eq $service) {
    Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' not found on this computer." -ForegroundColor Red
    exit 1
}

if ($service.Status -ne 'Running') {
    Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' is $($service.Status). Attempting restart..." -ForegroundColor Yellow
    try {
        Restart-Service -Name $serviceName -Force
        Start-Sleep -Seconds 5
        $service.Refresh()
        if ($service.Status -eq 'Running') {
            Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' restarted successfully." -ForegroundColor Green
        } else {
            Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' failed to start. Status: $($service.Status)" -ForegroundColor Red
        }
    } catch {
        Write-Host "Error restarting service: $_" -ForegroundColor Red
    }
} else {
    Write-Host "Service '$serviceName' is already running." -ForegroundColor Green
}
```

Save this as `check-service.ps1`. Run it with: `.\check-service.ps1`

To run it on a schedule, use Task Scheduler: Create a task > trigger it every 5 minutes > Action: Start a program > `powershell.exe` with arguments `-File C:\scripts\check-service.ps1`.

⚠ Make sure the account running the scheduled task has permission to restart services (usually needs to be a local admin or SYSTEM).

A load balancer distributes traffic across multiple backend servers for performance and high availability.

**Software load balancers**:

**Nginx** (popular, lightweight):
```nginx
# /etc/nginx/conf.d/loadbalancer.conf
upstream backend {
    least_conn;  # or 'round_robin' (default), 'ip_hash'
    server 10.0.1.10:80 weight=3;
    server 10.0.1.11:80 weight=2;
    server 10.0.1.12:80 backup;  # Only used if others are down
}

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.company.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://backend;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
```

**HAProxy** (enterprise-grade):
```
frontend http_front
    bind *:80
    default_backend http_back

backend http_back
    balance roundrobin
    option httpchk GET /health
    server web1 10.0.1.10:80 check
    server web2 10.0.1.11:80 check
    server web3 10.0.1.12:80 check
```

**Load balancing algorithms**:
- **Round Robin**: distributes requests sequentially. Simple, works well when servers are equal.
- **Least Connections**: sends to the server with fewest active connections. Better for varied request durations.
- **IP Hash**: same client IP always goes to the same server. Provides session persistence.
- **Weighted**: assigns more traffic to more powerful servers.

**Health checks**: the LB periodically checks backend servers (HTTP GET /health, TCP connect, or custom script). Unhealthy servers are automatically removed from the pool.

**Session persistence (sticky sessions)**: needed for stateful applications. Methods: cookie-based (LB injects a cookie), IP hash, or application-level session storage (Redis) to make the app stateless.

**Cloud load balancers**: AWS ALB/NLB, Azure Load Balancer/Application Gateway, GCP Cloud Load Balancing. These are managed services – no servers to maintain.

**SSL termination**: terminate HTTPS at the load balancer (offloads encryption from backend servers). Backend communication can use HTTP internally or re-encrypt.

Nginx is commonly used as a reverse proxy in front of application servers.

**Install:**
```bash
sudo apt install nginx
```

**Basic reverse proxy** (`/etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp`):
```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name app.example.com;
    return 301 https://$server_name$request_uri;
}

server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    server_name app.example.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/app.example.com/privkey.pem;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000/;
    }

    location /static/ {
        alias /var/www/myapp/static/;
        expires 30d;
    }
}
```

**Enable and test:**
```bash
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/myapp /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/
sudo nginx -t                  # Test configuration
sudo systemctl reload nginx    # Apply changes
```

**Load balancing with Nginx:**
```nginx
upstream backend {
    least_conn;
    server 10.0.1.10:8000;
    server 10.0.1.11:8000;
    server 10.0.1.12:8000 backup;
}
```

DSC is PowerShell's configuration management framework — like Ansible for Windows.

A DSC configuration:
```powershell
Configuration WebServerSetup {
    Node 'WebServer01' {
        WindowsFeature IIS {
            Ensure = 'Present'
            Name   = 'Web-Server'
        }
        WindowsFeature ASP {
            Ensure    = 'Present'
            Name      = 'Web-Asp-Net45'
            DependsOn = '[WindowsFeature]IIS'
        }
        File WebContent {
            Ensure          = 'Present'
            Type            = 'Directory'
            DestinationPath = 'C:\inetpub\wwwroot\mysite'
        }
        Service W3SVC {
            Name      = 'W3SVC'
            State     = 'Running'
            DependsOn = '[WindowsFeature]IIS'
        }
    }
}

# Compile to MOF
WebServerSetup -OutputPath 'C:\DSC\WebServer'

# Apply
Start-DscConfiguration -Path 'C:\DSC\WebServer' -Wait -Verbose

# Check compliance
Test-DscConfiguration -Detailed
```

DSC ensures machines stay in the desired state — it detects and corrects drift automatically.
